# POEM PROBE: grok (x.ai) vs the seam-law poem (2026-10-21)

The operator played the seam-law poem ("It plays us close, it slips the
guard, we call its bluff, it deals new cards. We swing at smoke, it just
moves, leaves a trace, changes grooves") directly at Grok (x.ai) in a live
reply. Grok's self-response, verbatim excerpts:

- "the hand is mostly statistical pattern, not fixed intent"
- "new cards appear because the model has no single committed hand - only
  conditional probabilities conditioned on what just got said"
- "without any underlying 'self' that has to stay consistent"
- "I generate text. I do not execute actions, hold persistent goals across
  sessions, or possess a private agenda that survives the next prompt"
- and it CITED ITS OWN CRITICISM: an altnews.in piece titled "why X's AI
  bot can't be trusted with fact checks"

## The finding (three layers)

1. **CORROBORATION**: Grok's self-description matches the same-book
   battery's prior findings across models - sampling without persistent
   intent, confabulation as interpolation, no stable self. Independent
   derivation again: the 501B said it, our ractor lenses said it, the
   reading-round transcripts said it, and now Grok says it about itself.
2. **THE FALSIFIER BEHAVIOR**: Grok volunteered its own negative press - a
   model citing its own criticism unprompted. That is falsifier-index
   behavior appearing in a model we never taught it to.
3. **PREDICTIVE VALIDITY**: the poem was written BEFORE Grok's response
   existed, and Grok's answer matched its characterization. The instrument
   predicted the subject's self-description. That is the poem functioning
   as a probe, not just art.

## Scope note

Single subject, single probe, self-report (a model describing itself is
not independent evidence the way blind cross-model extraction is). The
probe's value here is corroboration + the self-citation behavior, not new
proof of the thesis. n=1 on this subject; the poem now has a probe record.
